  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level trough is moving off to the east today with a \nshortwave dropping in out of the north tonight into tomorrow. \nWith colder air moving in at 850 mb and warm lakes, lake effect \nrain showers develop. The best chance for heavier rain showers \nis in CNY but there will be enough moisture and forcing that \nmuch of the region will at least see a few sprinkles with the \nshortwave passage tomorrow. Temperatures will be briefly cooler \ntomorrow before warming up Tuesday as warm air advection \ndevelops ahead of a deepening trough in the central US that will\nbring us more unsettled weather later this week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe potential for a Miller B noreaster is increasing as models \nare coming into better agreement with the northern and southern \n500 mb shortwaves phasing somewhere in the Ohio or Mississippi \nriver valleys. While the confidence in a stronger surface low \nhas increased, the track is still uncertain with a large spread \nof surface low locations Friday afternoon, ranging from western \nNY all the way too well off the Mid Atlantic coast. Most \nensemble members at this point do have at least some \nprecipitation moving through our area with an ensemble mean near\na half inch of QPF, with the 90th percentile around an inch to \ninch and a half. The caveat is that the cold airmass being \ningested into the storm from the north is slowly warming with \neach progressing model suits. This is not too surprising as \nthere is not much snow cover yet to the north so models are \nlikely overdoing the available cold air. While the confidence in\na stronger surface low has increased with a good soaking \nforecasted, the odds of a widespread heavier snow event has \ndecreased. There will still be enough cold air on the backside \nof the low so Friday night into Saturday may see a transition to\nsome wet, slushy snow. Accumulations likely will be light and \nwith a warm ground and air temps just around freezing, snow may \nmelt as fast as it is falling and limited to elevations above \n1000 feet.",
